
[38;2;154;140;198mLS(1)                     General Commands Manual                    LS(1)[0m

[38;2;224;184;255mNAME[0m
     ls – list directory contents

[38;2;224;184;255mSYNOPSIS[0m
     ls [[38;2;195;166;255m-@ABCFGHILOPRSTUWabcdefghiklmnopqrstuvwxy1%,[0m] [[38;2;195;166;255m--color[0m=when] [[38;2;195;166;255m-D[0m format] [file ...]

[38;2;224;184;255mDESCRIPTION[0m
     For each operand that names a file of type directory, ls displays the names
     of files contained within that directory, as well as any requested,
     associated information.

     If no operands are given, the contents of the current directory are
     displayed.  If more than one operand is given, non-directory operands are
     displayed first.

     The following options are available:

     [38;2;195;166;255m-a[0m      Include directory entries whose names begin with a dot (‘.’).

     [38;2;195;166;255m-l[0m      List files in the long format, as described in the The Long Format
             subsection below.

     [38;2;195;166;255m--color[0m=when
             Color output when it goes to a terminal.  The default is never.

[38;2;224;184;255mENVIRONMENT[0m
     The following environment variables affect the execution of ls:

     CLICOLOR  Use ANSI color sequences to distinguish file types.

[38;2;180;218;255m   The Long Format[0m
     If the [38;2;195;166;255m-l[0m option is given, the following information is displayed for each
     file: file mode, number of links, owner name, group name, and file name.

[38;2;224;184;255mFILES[0m
     Defaults are read from [38;2;251;243;200m~/.config/ls[0m and [38;2;251;243;200m/etc/ls.conf[0m, if they exist.

[38;2;224;184;255mSEE ALSO[0m
     [38;2;180;218;255mchmod[0m[38;2;242;212;155m(1)[0m, [38;2;180;218;255msort[0m[38;2;242;212;155m(1)[0m, [38;2;180;218;255mgetfacl[0m[38;2;242;212;155m(1)[0m, [38;2;180;218;255mxattr[0m[38;2;242;212;155m(7)[0m, [38;2;180;218;255msticky[0m[38;2;242;212;155m(7)[0m
     See [38;2;251;243;200mhttps://example.com/ls[0m for more.

